  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "What Is a Process, Really?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Program vs Process",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A program is bytes on disk. A process is that program plus its execution state — a running instance."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Address Space Map"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "text = code, data = initialised globals",
            "BSS = zero-initialised globals",
            "heap grows up, stack grows down",
            "mmap'd regions sit between the two",
            "every process gets its own private map"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Kernel View: task_struct"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Each process is a task_struct in the task list",
            "Holds PID, state, saved registers, credentials",
            "Plus fd table, signal handlers, mm pointer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. State Machine",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "STAT",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "R",
              "running / runnable"
            ],
            [
              "S",
              "interruptible sleep"
            ],
            [
              "D",
              "uninterruptible (disk I/O)"
            ],
            [
              "T",
              "stopped by signal"
            ],
            [
              "Z",
              "zombie, awaiting reap"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "I = idle kernel thread, never counted as load",
            "D-state task sits inside the kernel",
            "It checks no signals until the I/O returns"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "D-State Trap",
          "text": "This is the classic 'kill -9 does nothing' case — the task is blocked in kernel code, so the signal only lands once I/O finishes."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Identity Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "PID = self, PPID = parent",
            "PGID = process group, SID = session",
            "UID/EUID and GID/EGID drive permissions"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Threads Are Tasks"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A thread is a process sharing an address space",
            "Same TGID, different task id",
            "getpid() gives the TGID, gettid() the real task"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Introspection via /proc"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ls /proc/$$/          # status cmdline environ",
            "cat /proc/$$/maps     # address space layout",
            "ls /proc/$$/fd/       # open descriptors",
            "ps -eo pid,ppid,pgid,sid,stat,comm"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Keep This",
          "text": "The kernel schedules tasks, not programs. A 'process' is the accounting unit wrapped around one or more schedulable tasks."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "trap 'kill -TERM $child; wait $child' TERM",
            "grep -E 'SigBlk|SigIgn|SigCgt' /proc/$$/status"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "PIDs, PPIDs and the Process Tree",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Two Numbers",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "PID = positive integer assigned at creation",
            "PPID = PID of the creating parent",
            "Read them with getpid() and getppid()"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. PID 1 Is Special"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "init/systemd, started directly by the kernel",
            "Parent of every orphan on the box",
            "Ordinary signals cannot kill it",
            "If it dies the kernel panics"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "PID 0, Two Meanings",
          "items": [
            "The swapper/idle task, not userspace",
            "In kill(): pid 0 = my own process group"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Allocation and Wrap"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "PIDs are handed out sequentially, then wrap",
            "Ceiling is /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max",
            "Default 32768, raisable to 4194304"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "cat /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max   # 32768",
            "ps -p 4711 -o lstart=          # verify age"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Reuse Race",
          "text": "After a wrap, a PID you cached may now name a different process. Never kill an old saved PID without re-checking its start time."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Forest"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Strictly a forest rooted at PID 1",
            "pstree -p draws it with numbers",
            "ps -ejH prints an indented listing"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. PID Namespaces",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Viewpoint",
            "PID seen"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Inside container",
              "1"
            ],
            [
              "On the host",
              "24831"
            ],
            [
              "Reality",
              "one task, two numbers"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Your app becomes PID 1 inside the container",
            "So it inherits reaping duty for all orphans",
            "It must handle SIGTERM itself",
            "Otherwise the container never stops cleanly"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Finding Processes"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "pgrep -f 'worker --queue=mail'  # by cmdline",
            "pidof nginx                     # by name",
            "kill -0 4711 && echo alive      # probe"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Modern Handle",
          "text": "pidfd_open() returns a descriptor bound to that exact process; pidfd_send_signal() then targets it with no PID-reuse window."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Called Once, Returns Twice",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "pid_t fork(void) makes a near-identical copy of the caller. One call, two returning processes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "fork(): Cloning a Process",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Return",
            "Where you are"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "the child"
            ],
            [
              "> 0",
              "parent, value = child PID"
            ],
            [
              "-1",
              "failed, EAGAIN or ENOMEM"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Canonical Skeleton"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "pid_t p = fork();",
            "if (p < 0) perror(\"fork\");",
            "else if (p == 0) run_child();   // child",
            "else waitpid(p, &st, 0);        // parent"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. What Crosses Over"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Inherited",
            "Not inherited"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "open fds + offsets",
              "pending signals"
            ],
            [
              "environ, cwd, umask",
              "timers"
            ],
            [
              "signal dispositions",
              "file locks"
            ],
            [
              "resource limits",
              "the PID itself"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Child gets a fresh PID, PPID = parent's PID",
            "Dispositions survive, pending signals are cleared"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Copy-On-Write",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No physical copy of memory is made",
            "Pages are shared read-only after the fork",
            "A page is duplicated only when someone writes",
            "That is why forking a 4 GB process is cheap"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Ordering Is Undefined"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Never Assume Who Runs",
          "text": "Nothing guarantees parent or child goes first. If order matters, synchronise explicitly with a pipe, a lock or waitpid."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Shared Offsets Bite"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "printf(\"hello\\n\");   // still in buffer",
            "fflush(NULL);        // flush BEFORE fork",
            "fork();              // else printed twice"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Parent and child share one file offset per fd",
            "Concurrent writes to the same fd interleave"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Limits and Cousins",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            ":(){ :|:& };:    # fork bomb, do not run",
            "ulimit -u        # RLIMIT_NPROC ceiling"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Related Calls",
          "items": [
            "vfork(): borrows parent memory, legacy",
            "clone(): the real primitive, flag-driven",
            "posix_spawn(): fork+exec, cheap on big procs"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "exec(): Replacing the Program",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Same Process, New Program",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "exec overwrites the process image: same PID and PPID, brand new code, data, heap and stack."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Success Means No Return"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "execvp(\"ls\", argv);   // no return if ok",
            "perror(\"exec\");       // reached = failure",
            "_exit(127);           // 127 = not found"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Reading The Names"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Letter",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "l",
              "arg list, NULL-terminated"
            ],
            [
              "v",
              "arg vector array"
            ],
            [
              "p",
              "search $PATH"
            ],
            [
              "e",
              "explicit envp"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "execl, execv, execlp, execvp, execle, execve",
            "execve(path, argv, envp) is the only syscall",
            "Every other form is a libc wrapper on it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. argv[0] Is A Convention"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "It is not read from the filename",
            "Shells set it to the typed command name",
            "A login shell gets a leading '-' as the marker"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Crosses vs Resets",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Survives exec",
            "Wiped by exec"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "PID, PPID",
              "signal handlers"
            ],
            [
              "open fds (no CLOEXEC)",
              "memory mappings"
            ],
            [
              "cwd, umask, nice",
              "all other threads"
            ],
            [
              "PGID, session",
              "handler functions"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Ignored Stays Ignored",
          "text": "Handlers reset to SIG_DFL, but SIG_IGN carries across exec. A parent that ignored SIGINT hands you a child nobody can Ctrl-C."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Close-On-Exec"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "int fd = open(p, O_RDONLY | O_CLOEXEC);",
            "fcntl(fd, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC);  // or here",
            "// unmarked fds leak into every child"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Why Redirection Works",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "if (fork() == 0) {",
            "  dup2(fd, STDOUT_FILENO);  // rewire first",
            "  execvp(\"ls\", argv);       // then replace",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The new program simply sees stdout already set",
            "Shebang: kernel execs the interpreter instead",
            "#!/usr/bin/env python3 gets the script appended"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Death Needs A Witness",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A terminated child is not fully gone until the parent collects its status. That collection is reaping."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "child exits",
              "status kept",
              "parent waits",
              "slot freed"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "sh -c 'exit 42'; echo $?     # 42",
            "sh -c 'kill -9 $$'; echo $?  # 137 = 128+9",
            "false | true; echo ${PIPESTATUS[@]}  # 1 0"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "wait() Calls and Exit Codes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Three Calls"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "wait(&st) blocks for any child",
            "waitpid(pid, &st, 0) targets one child or group",
            "waitpid(-1, &st, WNOHANG) polls, returns 0 if none",
            "WNOHANG is what makes event loops possible"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Decoding Status"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Test",
            "Then read"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "WIFEXITED",
              "WEXITSTATUS = exit code"
            ],
            [
              "WIFSIGNALED",
              "WTERMSIG = killing signal"
            ],
            [
              "WIFSTOPPED",
              "WSTOPSIG = stopping signal"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Exit Codes Are 8 Bits",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "success"
            ],
            [
              "1-125",
              "application defined"
            ],
            [
              "126",
              "found, not executable"
            ],
            [
              "127",
              "command not found"
            ],
            [
              "128+N",
              "killed by signal N"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "SIGCHLD Reaping, Zombies and Orphans",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "143 = 128+15 = SIGTERM, 137 = SIGKILL",
            "$? holds the last status only",
            "${PIPESTATUS[@]} holds every pipeline stage"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. SIGCHLD And The Loop"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "void on_chld(int s) {",
            "  int st;",
            "  while (waitpid(-1, &st, WNOHANG) > 0)",
            "    ;   // drain, deliveries coalesce",
            "}"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Why The Loop",
          "text": "SIGCHLD is not queued. Four children dying at once can produce one delivery, so the handler must drain until waitpid returns 0 or -1."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Richer And Lazier"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "SIGCHLD fires on terminate, stop and continue",
            "waitid() returns a full siginfo_t",
            "waitid WNOWAIT peeks without reaping",
            "signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_IGN) auto-reaps, kills status"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What A Zombie Is",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "State Z, shown as <defunct>: exited, but the parent has not called wait. The kernel keeps only its status and accounting."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Real Cost"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No memory, no CPU, no scheduling",
            "One PID table slot each",
            "Failure mode is PID exhaustion, not RAM"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Can you kill a zombie?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "No — it is already dead",
            "kill -9 <zombie> is a plain no-op",
            "Make the parent reap it, or kill the parent",
            "Killing the parent re-parents it to PID 1"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "kill -9 8842      # nothing happens",
            "kill -TERM $(ps -o ppid= -p 8842)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "PID 1 waits in a loop and reaps immediately",
            "So the re-parented zombie disappears at once"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Orphans Are The Inverse",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "Zombie",
            "Orphan"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Alive?",
              "no",
              "yes"
            ],
            [
              "Parent",
              "alive, negligent",
              "dead"
            ],
            [
              "Fix",
              "reap it",
              "nothing, adopted"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An orphan is re-parented and keeps running",
            "It goes to init/systemd, or the nearest subreaper"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Subreapers, Daemons and Zombie Hunting",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Subreapers"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "c",
          "lines": [
            "prctl(PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER, 1);",
            "// adopts the whole descendant subtree"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "This is how systemd keeps a unit's tree",
            "Container managers use the same hook"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Double-Fork Daemonising"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "fork, then the parent exits right away",
            "Child calls setsid() for a new session",
            "It forks again and the middle process exits",
            "Grandchild is orphaned on purpose, init reaps it",
            "It can never acquire a controlling terminal"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Spotting Them",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm |",
            "  awk '$3 ~ /^Z/'   # list zombies",
            "top                 # zombie count line"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Container Fix",
          "text": "A shell or app as PID 1 that never waits piles up zombies from every subprocess. Use --init, tini, dumb-init, or a real reaper loop. Many zombies means a buggy parent — fix its SIGCHLD logic, don't schedule reboots."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. An Async Interrupt",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A signal is a small integer delivered to a process to notify it of an event — a software interrupt, not a message."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "generated",
              "pending",
              "delivered",
              "handled"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Generated by the kernel, another process, or itself",
            "Pending while blocked, never lost",
            "Handled according to its disposition"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Three Dispositions"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Disposition",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "SIG_DFL",
              "kernel default action"
            ],
            [
              "SIG_IGN",
              "discarded on delivery"
            ],
            [
              "handler fn",
              "your code runs"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Signal Model",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Default Actions"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Action",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Term",
              "process terminates"
            ],
            [
              "Core",
              "terminates + dumps core"
            ],
            [
              "Ign",
              "discarded"
            ],
            [
              "Stop",
              "suspended"
            ],
            [
              "Cont",
              "resumed"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Queued vs Collapsed",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Standard signals 1-31 are NOT queued",
            "Two arrivals while blocked collapse into one",
            "SIGRTMIN..SIGRTMAX (32-64) ARE queued",
            "Real-time signals arrive in order",
            "sigqueue() can attach a value to each"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Blocking, Not Losing"
        },
